<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <span class="eyebrow">Guide</span>
    <h1>Finding fees that were never recorded at all</h1>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>For the owner and the principal</span>
      <span>About 6 minutes</span>
    </div>

    <p>
      Most anti-fraud advice for schools is about receipts: number them, never
      delete them, keep the book. That advice is right and it catches one kind of
      problem. It cannot catch the other kind, and the other kind is bigger.
    </p>

    <h2>The two kinds, and why one is invisible</h2>
    <p>
      <b>Under-recording</b> is when a payment is entered for less than was
      taken. A parent pays Rs 5,800, the receipt says Rs 3,800. Gapless receipt
      numbers and a copy of the receipt going to the parent catch this, because
      two records of the same event exist and they disagree.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Non-recording</b> is when the payment is never entered at all. A parent
      pays Rs 5,800 in cash, no receipt is written, and the money does not reach
      the drawer. There is now exactly one record of the event, in the parent's
      memory, and nothing in the school's books to compare it against. Your
      receipt book is perfect. Your day book balances. The month's collection is
      simply smaller than it should have been, and nothing in the system knows
      what "should have been" is.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        A perfectly balanced set of books proves that what was written down is
        consistent. It says nothing at all about what was not written down.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>The check that does work</h2>
    <p>
      Compare what the <b>roll</b> says should have been billed against what was
      actually collected, class by class, and look at the difference. That is it.
      The power of it is that the expected figure comes from a completely
      different source than the collected figure: the roll and the fee structure,
      not the receipt book.
    </p>
    <p>
      For one class the arithmetic is:
    </p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Line</th><th>Where it comes from</th><th>Example</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Pupils on the roll</td><td>The admission register</td><td>34</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Monthly fee for the class</td><td>The fee structure</td><td>Rs 3,750</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Approved concessions</td><td>The discount register</td><td>Rs 5,200</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Expected</b></td><td>34 x 3,750 minus 5,200</td><td><b>Rs 122,300</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Collected this month</td><td>The receipt book</td><td>Rs 96,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Gap</b></td><td>Expected minus collected</td><td><b>Rs 26,300</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>A gap is a question, not an accusation</h2>
    <p>
      This matters, and getting it wrong will cost you a good clerk. In every
      school we have looked at, most of the gap is one of these, and none of them
      is theft:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <b>Pupils who left and were never marked as gone.</b> They are still on
        the roll, so they are still being billed, and no money will ever arrive.
        This is usually the single largest slice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Concessions granted verbally.</b> The principal told a family to pay
        half and nobody wrote it in the discount register, so the expected figure
        is too high by the amount of the kindness.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Families who are simply behind.</b> Genuine arrears, already known to
        the office, correctly recorded.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>A wrong fee amount on the class.</b> The structure says Rs 3,750 and
        the school has been charging Rs 3,500 since the last increase was agreed.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Work down that list before you think about the fifth possibility. And when
      you find one of the first four, the fix is a record: mark the leaver as
      left, write the concession in the register, correct the fee amount. Each one
      you fix makes the next month's gap a sharper instrument.
    </p>

    <h3>The ghost check, which needs no software</h3>
    <p>
      Take the list of pupils being billed this month. Take the attendance
      register for the same month. Any pupil on the first list who appears on no
      day of the second is worth a five minute conversation. They have almost
      certainly left. You are billing a family that has moved on, and your
      expected figure is carrying money that will never arrive.
    </p>

    <h2>How often to run it</h2>
    <p>
      Monthly, at the same point in the month, and write the figure down. One
      month's gap tells you very little. Six months of gaps tells you whether the
      number is drifting, and in which class, which is the thing you actually
      want to know.
    </p>

    <h2>How this works in The School Manager</h2>
    <p>
      The expected against collected table is a screen, computed from the roll
      and the fee structure rather than typed in, with the shortfall per class and
      the ghost check beside it. Concessions cannot be granted without an approver
      and a reason, which removes the second cause above entirely, and a leaver
      marked as left stops being billed the same day.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is worth being clear that no software can find non-recording on its own.
      What it can do is compute the expected figure honestly, from a source the
      person taking the cash does not control, and put the two numbers next to
      each other every month without anybody having to remember.
    </p>
